        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> However, the line before will always have the indentation, and I don't know if I can get rid of this with the current settings. I tried to replace the whole line with the same without indent, before returning `{ left, right }`, and it does funny but irrelevant things.\n\nYes, indeed, modifying the text inside those functions is not something with a guarantee to work with 'mini.surround'. The behavior of ignoring leading/trailing whitespace of a linewise selection is (I think) a more common outcome than always putting on the first/last column.\n\nWhat I [recently realized](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1898#issuecomment-3089276534) is that the best way to have this working is probably to force charwise selection instead of linewise. So for example to surrounding current line with empty lines: `0v$` - `sa` - `<C-j>`. If this is a common occurrence, creating custom charwise textobjects might be a worthwhile.\n\n---\n\nThat said, I now wonder if it might be better to drop this whitespace adjustment for the surrounding that contains/starts/ends with a newline. Specifically for these multiline surrounding cases. I'll think about it.",
