        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> What I [recently realized](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/1898#issuecomment-3089276534) is that the best way to have this working is probably to force charwise selection instead of linewise. So for example to surrounding current line with empty lines: `0v$` - `sa` - `<C-j>`. If this is a common occurrence, creating custom charwise textobjects might be a worthwhile.\n\nAt first I was replying to this more recent issue, but it was for a specific markdown use case, I thought my answer would better fit here.\n\nThat said, I'm glad I did it because you explained what you said in the other thread a bit differently and I understand it now ^^\n\nI had a keymap `ss` to `sa_`, and now I see the main difference with your example, `sa_` takes the line from the first non-space character.\n\nRebinding `ss` to your suggestion `0v$sa` works perfectly, and I kept `['\\r'] = { output = { left = '\\n', right = '\\n' } }` to use `Return` intsead of `C-j` which is… less natural.",
